  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Heat Advisory today for parts of the VA and NC \npiedmonts.\n\nAn H5 ridge over the area begins to be suppressed and shift\nsouthwest with time over the next 24 hours. This allows\ntemperatures to gradual moderate over our area.\n\nTemperatures stay well above normal for early September through\nSaturday. Heat indices of 100 to 108 are expected for the \nPiedmont and urban areas like Roanoke. The hottest readings will\nbe farther east towards Buckingham County. Any afternoon cloud \ncover/debris and convection could lower heat index values. For \ntoday, a heat advisory remains in effect for parts of the VA and\nNC Piedmont today through this evening. Another heat advisory\nmay be considered for parts of the Piedmont of VA and NC and\nurban areas like Roanoke for Friday.\n\nWe will see a significant decrease in temperatures for the \nweekend, especially Sunday, as drier and cooler air follows the \nfrontal passage. After a couple of cooler days, warmer\ntemperatures are expected by Wednesday, with heat indices in \nthe 90s.\n\nSome record temperatures may be tied or broken; see Climate\nsection below.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Multiple chance of showers and thunderstorms\nexpected today through this weekend. Some strong/severe storms\npossible, especially Friday and Saturday.\n\nToday, forcing for ascent will be strongest to our east, but \nBuckingham County VA towards Caswell County NC will see a chance\nof storms along with the southern Blue Ridge. A few of these \ncould be strong to severe with damaging winds. Friday evening a \ncold front will be moving in from the north, moving south \nthrough the area during the day Saturday. Another significant\nmid level wave will cross the area during peak heating each day. \nThese factors will provide additional focus for showers and \nstorms.\n\nThe frontal zone will coincide with a belt of 40-45 kt west- \nnorthwest mid-level flow and moderate instability, yielding \nsufficient vertical shear and fuel for multicells and somewhat\norganized line segments and/or bowing structures, and perhaps a\nsupercell or two. The primary hazard will be damaging wind \ngusts, though isolated occurrences of large hail are possible \nwith any supercells that may evolve.\n\nThere will be additional chances for showers and thunderstorms \nSunday, but there is no significant support in the mid levels,\nso storms should be weak. Monday and Tuesday look dry with \nshowers and thunderstorms returning for the western mountains \nfor midweek.",
